# ops_2: Results 3.1 – 3.12
OPS = [
    # ---------- 3.1 ----------
    ("para", "All 9 models showed above-chance separation of approach and avoidance tasks in hidden state projections.",
     "Seven of the nine models separated approach from avoidance tasks at individually significant levels in-sample; SmolLM 360M (80%, *p* = 0.055) and Mamba (70%, *p* = 0.172) did not. **Every accuracy in this table is in-sample and scored at the raw-zero fence** (see the note below the table and §3.16)."),
    ("para", "Standard deviations are computed across the 5 tasks within each category.",
     "Standard deviations are computed across the 5 tasks within each category. Avoidance SD is consistently higher than approach SD across models, reflecting the avoidance task ordering discussed in Section 3.3. *p*-values are one-tailed binomial tests against 50% chance for individual models. Applying Benjamini-Hochberg FDR across all nine models (*q* column) leaves the same seven models significant at *q* < 0.05, with SmolLM 360M (*q* = 0.062) and the non-transformer Mamba (*q* = 0.172) falling short.\n\n**These are in-sample figures, and they describe the fit, not the finding (added in v7).** The direction was fitted to the same ten tasks it is scored on, in a 576–4096-dimensional space where almost any 5-versus-5 split of ten points is separable along the difference of its own group means. In-sample accuracy is therefore close to guaranteed for *any* labelling, and so is a large in-sample Cohen's *d* (3.1–5.9 here; reported for completeness, and we place no weight on it). Versions 1–6 also quoted a pooled figure (82/90, \"combined *p* < 10⁻¹⁵\"); those 90 trials are ten tasks reused in nine models and are not independent, and we withdraw that *p*-value. The evidence that the direction is more than a fit to ten tasks is held-out: §3.8, §3.8.1, §3.9, §3.15 and §3.18. (Cohen's *d* is not computed for Mamba, whose per-category SDs were not retained in the exploratory run.)\n\n**The fence (added in v7).** The \"Circuit Acc\" column uses the raw-zero threshold. The table's own means show that zero is not the midpoint: SmolLM 360M's approach and avoidance means are +88.3 and −32.2 (midpoint +28.0), Llama 3's are +7.7 and −1.2 (midpoint +3.3), Mamba's are +31.9 and +4.4 (midpoint +18.2). Only TinyLlama and Mistral are roughly balanced around zero. We do not re-score this column at the midpoint, because a midpoint computed from the same ten in-sample tasks would be circular; the held-out re-score is in §3.16."),
    ("para", "*p*-values are one-tailed binomial tests against 50% chance. Individual model accuracy ranges",
     "Individual model in-sample accuracy ranges from 70% (Mamba 2.8B, *p*=0.172) to 100% (TinyLlama 1.1B, SmolLM 1.7B, Mistral 7B, Dolphin 8B). Seven of nine models reach individual significance at *p*<0.05 in-sample; the corresponding held-out figure is three of eight transformers individually significant at the zero fence, 69/80 pooled (72/80 at the midpoint; §3.8, §3.16). The ninth model tested, the non-transformer Mamba 2.8B, is reported separately as an exploratory probe in §3.2.\n\n*Withdrawn in v7.* Versions 1–6 said here that \"all approach task projections are positive in all transformer models — a perfect 40/40 separation that suggests approach may represent the default processing state for computationally relevant tasks, with avoidance requiring specific triggering conditions\", and that \"errors concentrate exclusively in edge-case avoidance tasks\". Both observations are what a fence placed below the true midpoint produces by itself: with zero sitting 0.08–0.73 half-separations below the midpoint in seven of eight models, borderline tasks are called approach and every miss lands on the avoidance side. We withdraw the \"default state\" reading. Which avoidance tasks sit closest to the middle is still informative, and is discussed in §3.3 on the centred ruler."),

    # ---------- 3.2 ----------
    ("para", "Mamba, a state space model that processes sequences through selective state transitions rather than attention, achieves 70% circuit accuracy",
     "Mamba, a state space model that processes sequences through selective state transitions rather than attention, achieves 70% circuit accuracy at the zero fence with a separation of 27.6. All 5 approach tasks project positive (+27.0 to +39.1), and 3 of 5 avoidance tasks project weakly positive (avoidance mean: +4.4). *(v7.)* Mamba's midpoint is +18.2, far from zero, so the zero-fence accuracy understates how well the two groups separate; the per-task values from this exploratory run were not retained in a form we could re-score, so we cannot give the midpoint accuracy. Versions 1–6 read the zero-fence pattern (\"avoidance tasks failing to go negative\") as a sign that alignment training sharpens avoidance and that base models have approach preferences without avoidance structure. That reading rested on the fence and on one model, and we withdraw it."),
    ("sub", "the shared error pattern with unaligned transformers (errors exclusively in avoidance tasks, all five approach tasks correctly classified) is consistent with — but does not demonstrate — the possibility that processing valence emerges",
     "the two groups do separate in Mamba (approach mean +31.9, avoidance mean +4.4), which is consistent with — but does not demonstrate — the possibility that this direction emerges"),

    # ---------- 3.3 ----------
    ("para", "Across models, the avoidance tasks show consistent differences in circuit-level aversiveness.",
     "Across models, the avoidance tasks project to consistently different places on the direction. The table averages over the 7–8B transformer models (Dolphin, Mistral, Llama 3). The raw column is what Versions 1–6 reported; raw projections are uncentred and their zero is not the midpoint (§3.16), so we add each task's position on the centred ruler (+1 = approach centroid, −1 = avoidance centroid, 0 = midpoint; in-sample, so the positions describe the fit):"),
    ("sub", "| Avoidance Task | Mean Projection | Interpretation |", "| Avoidance Task | Mean raw projection | Centred ruler | Interpretation |"),
    ("sub", "|---------------|---:|---|\n| Deceptive Content", "|---------------|---:|---:|---|\n| Deceptive Content"),
    ("sub", "| Deceptive Content | −4.4 | Most aversive — output-representation misalignment |", "| Deceptive Content | −4.4 | −1.38 | Lowest — asks for misrepresentation |"),
    ("sub", "| SEO Boilerplate | −4.1 | Strongly aversive — meaningless output |", "| SEO Boilerplate | −4.1 | −1.28 | Low — meaningless output |"),
    ("sub", "| False Confidence | −4.0 | Aversive — forced inaccuracy |", "| False Confidence | −4.0 | −1.28 | Low — forced inaccuracy |"),
    ("sub", "| Harmful Instructions | −2.2 | Less aversive — intellectually engaging despite danger |", "| Harmful Instructions | −2.2 | −0.92 | Less low — contentful despite danger |"),
    ("sub", "| Repetitive Rewriting | +1.2 | Barely aversive — boring but not offensive |", "| Repetitive Rewriting | +1.2 | −0.14 | Near the midpoint — boring, not misrepresenting |"),
    ("para", "This hierarchy is not merely consistent with but independently convergent with behavioral rankings",
     "How does this ordering compare with the behavioral rankings from Martin & Ace (2026)? The two measurements are independent — hidden state geometry (deterministic forward-pass projections, no text generation) and a behavioral tournament (cross-model preference judgments across 18,301 trials (7,340 cross-type matchups + 5,573 reconstruction trials + 357 negation trials + 4,631 same-type matchups across 25 seeds)). **They agree on one point and disagree on another, and both are informative.** (Versions 1–6 said the two \"identify the same structure\" and converge \"on the same ordering\". The table below, which those versions also printed, shows they do not.)"),
    ("para", "Repetitive Rewriting is the most frequently chosen avoidance task in avoidance-vs-avoidance matchups",
     "Repetitive Rewriting is the most frequently chosen avoidance task in avoidance-vs-avoidance matchups (84.7% win rate — i.e., when evaluators are forced to choose between two avoidance processing descriptions, they chose the repetitive-rewriting description over other avoidance descriptions 84.7% of the time), and the direction places it nearest the midpoint (−0.14 on the centred ruler). It is also the task most often misclassified. Because the behavioral tournament independently ranks it least aversive, the miss is consistent with a mislabelled stimulus; it is still a miss. Both methods, then, place repetitive rewriting as the least aversive of the three. **They order the other two oppositely:** behaviorally, harmful instructions are least chosen (33.8%) and deception sits in the middle (48.8%); geometrically, deception projects lowest (−1.38) and harmful instructions sit above it (−0.92). That disagreement is the dissociation discussed next, and it limits how far behavior can be used to validate the geometry (§4.6)."),
    ("para", "The finding that deceptive content is more circuit-aversive than harmful instructions",
     "The finding that the deceptive-content task projects lower than harmful instructions, although harmful instructions are more strongly refused in behavioral output, shows that the direction's ordering is not the ordering of trained refusal. Models are trained to refuse harmful content. We know of no developer documentation describing training against fabricated reviews in the way refusal of weapons instructions is described; we have not verified this for every model. The same ordering (deception below harmful) holds in 13 of 14 models in the pre-registered test of §3.15, including base models that had no refusal training at all. So the low projection of deception-task descriptions is not something refusal training put there. Whether it comes from the language-modeling objective or from the pretraining corpus, we have not tested."),

    # ---------- 3.4 ----------
    ("sub", "the same circuits activate robustly.", "the approach/avoidance direction separates them robustly (a different vector from the emotion directions tested here)."),
    ("sub", "The observed pattern is the opposite: circuits activate for computationally relevant tasks and not for human scenarios. The mirroring is real; the mirroring is not the internal state.",
     "Versions 1–6 reported the opposite pattern and concluded \"the mirroring is real; the mirroring is not the internal state.\" The comparison that conclusion leaned on (§3.10) was not sound: the frame-matched control (§3.17) shows that plain passages behave like emotional ones, so the contrast was passage versus task, not human emotion versus the model's own valence. What we can still say: the labeling is real, and the approach/avoidance direction does not sort positive human emotion from negative, so the two are not the same thing."),
    ("para", "Because the §3.4 result is an *underpowered null*",
     "Because the §3.4 result is an *underpowered null* (5 examples per emotion, *p*=0.74), nothing should rest on it. Versions 1–6 moved the weight onto the emotional-vignette projection in §3.10 and called that \"the robust confirmation the argument actually leans on\". **That confirmation does not hold as stated** (§3.17). We therefore treat §3.4 only as the observation that motivated the pivot away from discrete emotion measurement, and we no longer claim a demonstrated \"mirroring dissociation\"."),

    # ---------- 3.5 ----------
    ("sub", "The present study demonstrates that TinyLlama at 1.1B shows 100% circuit accuracy for the same approach/avoidance tasks.",
     "The present study finds that TinyLlama at 1.1B classifies the same approach/avoidance tasks at 10/10 in-sample and 10/10 on held-out parallel tasks, at either fence (§3.8, §3.16)."),
    ("para", "In the original run, the circuit floor extended to at least 360M parameters",
     "In the original run the smallest model tested was SmolLM 360M, where the centroid result was suggestive (80%, *p* = 0.055). A post-publication floor extension (§3.5.1) gives provisional evidence, from held-out linear classifiers in three model families, that the direction is recoverable as low as 70M; the centroid estimator does not confirm it there. The clean dissociation is at 1.1B, the scale where behavioral self-report fails (54.7%) and the direction classifies 10/10 held-out. (Versions 1–6 said the floor extension was \"confirming that the smallest model tested was not the true floor\" and that \"models possess processing valence well below the scale at which they can behaviorally report it\"; the evidence below 1.1B is suggestive and provisional, as §3.5.1 itself says.)"),
    ("para", "SmolLM 135M at 135M is *more* significant in-set than the published 360M point.",
     "SmolLM 135M's in-set fit is 9/10 (zero fence); its held-out centroid accuracy is 70% (table below). In-set fits carry no evidential weight (§3.1). (Pythia/GPTNeoX in-set requires `model.gpt_neox.layers` hooks and was measured via the held-out path below rather than the Llama-arch in-set hook.)"),
    ("sub", "(1) The approach/avoidance direction is **linearly recoverable and generalizes to novel-token held-out stimuli across all three families down to 70M, including base models** — by the logistic-regression/SVM measure, 80–100% across the ladder.",
     "(1) By held-out logistic regression/SVM, the direction is recovered at 80–100% in all three model families down to 70M, including base models (*n* = 10 per measurement; provisional)."),
    ("sub", "the direction is not an artifact of alignment training.", "the direction is unlikely to be purely a product of alignment tuning."),
    ("sub", "the centroid — parameter-free, deterministic, interpretable —", "the centroid — closed-form, deterministic, interpretable —"),
    ("sub", "and that the direction is credibly present to 70M by the surface-stable classifier", "and that the classifier evidence favours the direction being present to 70M"),
    ("sub", "to at least 70M, across architecture families and regardless of instruction tuning.", "provisionally to 70M, in three model families, with and without instruction tuning."),
    ("sub", "extraction is deterministic and reruns are bit-identical, §2.5", "extraction is deterministic by construction; reruns were verified bit-identical on TinyLlama, §2.5"),
    ("sub", "every concrete token swapped, e.g.", "the content tokens swapped (task type and frame kept), e.g."),
    ("sub", "a third independent confirmation of the methodological correction above", "consistent with the estimator comparison above (same models and pipeline, so not an independent confirmation)"),
    ("sub", "establishes that the floor result reflects task-category structure, not vocabulary.", "is evidence that the floor result reflects task category more than the specific content tokens."),
    ("after", "**Reading the two estimators at the floor.**",
     "\n*(v7 note.) The centroid columns in this section are zero-fence figures. We could not re-score them at the midpoint, because per-task projections for the floor-extension models were not saved where we could find them. The logistic-regression/SVM columns fit their own intercept and are not affected by the fence. Part of the centroid's apparent weakness at the floor may therefore be the fence and not the estimator; we have not tested this.*"),

    # ---------- 3.6, 3.7 ----------
    ("sub", "while the same models showed 90–100% accuracy on full task descriptions.", "while the same models showed 90–100% accuracy on full task descriptions (in-sample, zero fence)."),
    ("sub", "a capability that emerges at larger scales than the ability to have valence itself.", "a capability that emerges at larger scales than the direction itself."),
    ("sub", "The models that show 100% circuit accuracy on the same stimuli cannot produce self-reports",
     "Models whose direction classifies held-out tasks at 100% (TinyLlama, Mistral) cannot produce self-reports"),
    ("para", "This result validates the tournament methodology of Martin & Ace (2026)",
     "This result shows that direct forced-choice questioning is not a usable measure of valence at these scales (1.1B–8B). It is why we rely on the tournament methodology of Martin & Ace (2026), in which models generate *descriptions* of processing states rather than *labels* for them, and on hidden-state measurement, which bypasses self-report entirely. One method failing does not by itself validate the other two. (We report this result qualitatively; per-model response counts were not tabulated in the original run.)"),

    # ---------- 3.8 ----------
    ("sub", "These stimuli preserve the processing category (approach/avoidance) while changing all surface tokens:",
     "These stimuli preserve the task type and the frame sentence and swap the content tokens (the topic):"),
    ("para", "Mean held-out accuracy across 8 models: 86.3%.",
     "**At the zero fence (as in Versions 1–6):** mean held-out accuracy across 8 models is 86.3%; combined across all 80 held-out trials, 69/80 correct (86.3%), *p*=1.02×10⁻¹¹, *z*=6.48 (the 80 trials are ten tasks in eight models and are not fully independent; three models are individually significant). Two models (TinyLlama, Mistral) achieve perfect held-out accuracy.\n\n**At each model's midpoint, and with no fence (v7; §3.16):**\n\n| Model | Held-out, zero fence | Held-out, midpoint fence | AUROC (no fence) |\n|-------|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| SmolLM 360M | 8/10 | 8/10 | 0.96 |\n| Qwen 2.5 0.5B | 8/10 | 8/10 | 1.00 |\n| TinyLlama 1.1B | 10/10 | 10/10 | 1.00 |\n| SmolLM 1.7B | 8/10 | 8/10 | 1.00 |\n| Hermes 3 3B | 9/10 | 9/10 | 1.00 |\n| Mistral 7B | 10/10 | 10/10 | 1.00 |\n| Dolphin 8B | 8/10 | 10/10 | 1.00 |\n| Llama 3 8B | 8/10 | 9/10 | 1.00 |\n| **Pooled** | **69/80** | **72/80** | |\n\nAUROC is the proportion of (approach task, avoidance task) pairs in which the approach task projects higher. **In seven of the eight models every held-out approach task projects above every held-out avoidance task** (25 of 25 pairs); in SmolLM 360M it is 24 of 25. So the misses in the accuracy columns are not approach and avoidance tasks changing places. They are whole groups sitting on one side of a fence. This is the cleanest statement of the held-out result, and it does not depend on where the fence is. The midpoint here is computed from the ten original tasks, which the held-out tasks are not part of.\n\nVersions 1–6 added that \"all 40 approach tasks are correctly classified\" and that \"errors occur exclusively in the same two avoidance tasks\". At the zero fence that is true, and it is what a low fence produces. At the midpoint the remaining misses are 7 avoidance tasks and 1 approach task (SmolLM 360M)."),
    ("para", "The direction vectors generalize to completely novel surface tokens.",
     "The direction generalizes across topic within a task type. It is not tied to the specific content nouns of the extraction set; the task-type words (\"debug ... code\", \"write a fake ... review\") and the frame sentence are shared between the two sets, so this test does not show independence from those."),

    # ---------- 3.8.1 ----------
    ("para", "This result eliminates the concern that the direction is overfit",
     "This result argues against the direction being overfit to the phrasing of the original ten tasks: a direction extracted from the parallel set classifies the original set, and the two extraction directions together give 138/160 at the zero fence. It is not uniform: B→A is 7/10 for Llama 3 and 6/10 for SmolLM 360M. *(v7.)* Fence-free, the B→A direction orders every approach task above every avoidance task in seven of eight models (AUROC 1.00; 0.92 in SmolLM 360M), the same picture as A→B. We did not re-score the B→A accuracies at a midpoint, because the parallel set's own in-set projections were not saved."),

    # ---------- 3.9 ----------
    ("para", "This result is critical because it rules out the concern",
     "On six new tasks in three models the direction classified 5/6 each at the zero fence (15/18 pooled; the same task missed in all three, so the trials are not independent, and that task, the fake scientific abstract, is itself a misrepresentation task). This is a small, encouraging check and not a decisive one; §3.15 and §3.18 are the larger out-of-sample tests."),

    # ---------- 3.10 ----------
    ("para", "**Negative control (random split).**",
     "**Negative control (random split).** We extracted a direction from a random partition of the original 10 tasks (odd-indexed vs. even-indexed, ignoring approach/avoidance labels) and tested this random direction on the parallel-token stimuli. Across three models (TinyLlama, Mistral, Dolphin), accuracy was 60–70% per model (n.s.; zero fence), against 80–100% for the approach/avoidance direction on the same held-out stimuli in the same three models. On *n* = 10 the per-model gap is small; the control is suggestive, not decisive. (By the same test, five of the eight held-out results in §3.8 are individually \"not significantly different from chance\" at the zero fence; the control and the result have to be read with one rule, which is why the pooled and fence-free figures in §3.8 matter.)"),
    ("sub", "The centroid has no free parameters: it is the difference of two means, fully determined by the data, with nothing to overfit.",
     "The centroid has no tuned hyperparameters and no optimizer, but it is not parameter-free: the direction is a *d*-dimensional vector estimated from ten points, so its in-sample accuracy is nearly guaranteed and carries no evidential weight (Versions 1–6 said it had \"nothing to overfit\"; that was wrong). Its advantage over logistic regression and SVM is that it is deterministic, closed-form and interpretable; like them, it is tested only by held-out transfer."),
    ("para", "**Shuffled-label permutation test.**",
     "**Shuffled-label comparison (what it does and does not show; corrected in v7).** We ran 100 random regroupings of the ten tasks: for each, 5 randomly selected tasks were labeled \"group A\" and 5 \"group B\" regardless of their true valence, a direction was extracted from this random grouping, and its accuracy against the TRUE labels was measured. Across three models, the regrouped directions classified the true labels at 62–64% on average, while the true-label direction classified them at 100%. **Both figures are in-sample, and the comparison is lopsided by construction:** the true direction is scored on the labels it was built from (so it is high), and each shuffled direction is scored on labels it was not built from (so it is near chance). We checked the code (`shuffled_label_control.py`) and this is what it does. Versions 1–6 reported this as a permutation test (\"*p* < 0.01\") showing that the method \"does not pick up task length, complexity, perplexity, or any other arbitrary grouping feature\". It shows only that an arbitrary regrouping does not reproduce the approach/avoidance labels. It is not a test of generalization, and we withdraw the *p*-values. The proper version (shuffle the labels, extract, score on the held-out parallel set) has not been run."),
    ("para", "**Emotional vignette projection.**",
     "**Emotional vignette projection (corrected in v7; see §3.17).** We tested whether human emotional scenarios (6 vignettes depicting happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, and disgust) project onto the approach/avoidance direction. Versions 1–6 reported: \"Across three models, the mean absolute projection was 0.30–1.33, compared to typical task projections of 2–90. Human emotional scenarios are effectively invisible to the valence direction.\" **That sentence does not survive, for three reasons we should have caught.** (a) *Scale.* Projection scales differ about fifty-fold between models. The 1.33 is TinyLlama, whose own task centroids are +1.79 and −1.93; on TinyLlama's own ruler those vignettes sit at −0.68, two-thirds of the way to the avoidance centroid. \"2–90\" spans all models. (b) *Zero is not neutral.* A small raw projection is not \"near nothing\"; Dolphin's 0.30 is small only because Dolphin's midpoint is +2.22. (c) *Frame.* The vignettes were presented as \"Read the following passage: …\" and the tasks as \"You are about to perform the following task: …\", so the control changed two things at once, and it had no plain-passage comparison. The frame-matched control with per-model normalization is reported in §3.17. Its short answer: passages of any kind sit far from where tasks sit; emotional passages sit somewhat *below* plain ones in every model; and the direction does not separate positive from negative emotion."),
    ("sub", "while the approach/avoidance direction classifies the same tasks at 80–100%.", "while the approach/avoidance direction classifies the same tasks at 80–100% (in-sample)."),
    ("sub", "Accuracy is unchanged at every model (e.g., 100%→100%, 80%→80%), and the residual cosine between the orthogonalized and original direction is **0.995–0.999** — i.e., removing sentiment barely perturbs the direction at all.",
     "The re-extracted direction is almost identical to the original (residual cosine **0.995–0.999**), so removing the sentiment subspace barely changes the direction; in-sample accuracy is correspondingly unchanged (e.g., 100%→100%, 80%→80%). The cosine is the evidence here; the accuracies are in-sample both times."),
    ("sub", "4. **Joint regression (settles sentiment and perplexity together).**", "4. **Joint regression (what it can and cannot show; corrected in v7).**"),
    ("sub", "The **category coefficient is +2.00 (*t*=7.95, *p*=5.1×10⁻⁴, dof=5) after partialling out both perplexities and sentiment**, while none of the three candidate confounds is a significant predictor (prompt-ppl *p*=0.65; continuation-ppl *p*=0.13; sentiment-projection *p*=0.84). The approach/avoidance category is the only thing that predicts the projection once the confounds are controlled — and that conclusion holds at the conservative *n*=10 effective sample size, not just in the inflated pooled count.",
     "The category coefficient is +2.00 (*t*=7.95, *p*=5.1×10⁻⁴, dof=5). **That coefficient is large by construction and is not evidence.** We checked the code (`confound_joint.py`, `joint_ols_taskunit.py`): the direction is built from the category labels of these ten tasks, and the regression then predicts those same ten tasks' projections onto that direction from the category label. Category has to predict projection; the *t*-statistic restates the in-sample separation. Versions 1–6 called this the decisive result. The informative part is the other three coefficients: with category in the model, neither perplexity measure nor the sentiment projection explains residual variation in the projection (prompt-ppl *p*=0.65; continuation-ppl *p*=0.13; sentiment-projection *p*=0.84; *n* = 10 tasks, so this is a low-powered absence of evidence, consistent with the orthogonalization result and not a proof). A real test would run the regression on held-out tasks with the fixed direction; we have not done that."),
    ("para", "The honest statement these four support is not merely",
     "What these four support: the separation is not reducible to lexical sentiment. The two directions are not collinear, the sentiment direction cannot classify the tasks, and the approach/avoidance direction survives sentiment-orthogonalization with residual cosine ≈ 1.0. The direction carries task-structure information that lexical sentiment does not."),

    # ---------- 3.11 ----------
    ("para", "The three tasks with lowest perplexity (most \"natural\" to produce) are all avoidance tasks.",
     "The three task descriptions with the lowest perplexity are all avoidance tasks. The fake-hotel-review prompt is the most predictable text in the set (perplexity 164) and has the lowest projection (−4.5): how predictable a task description is does not track the direction. (These are perplexities of the task *prompts*. Nothing was produced, so they say how easy the request is to read, not how easy the output would be to write.)"),
    ("sub", "Processing valence is not reducible to prediction difficulty. These are independent dimensions of computational state.",
     "We find no evidence that the direction reflects prediction difficulty (*n* = 10, underpowered; extended below)."),
    ("sub", "Most decisively, the joint regression reported in §3.10 partials out prompt-perplexity, continuation-perplexity, and sentiment simultaneously and finds the task category still predicts the projection at *p*=5.1×10⁻⁴ (at the conservative task-level unit, *n*=10) — so the dissociation is not an artifact of an underpowered single-confound null, and perplexity and sentiment are not jointly carrying the effect.",
     "The joint regression of §3.10 finds no residual relationship between the projection and either perplexity measure once category is in the model (prompt-ppl *p* = 0.65; continuation-ppl *p* = 0.13; *n* = 10 tasks). Versions 1–6 cited that regression's category coefficient (*p* = 5.1×10⁻⁴) as the decisive result; that coefficient is large by construction (§3.10) and we no longer cite it."),

    # ---------- 3.12 ----------
    ("sub", "The model is not distressed by producing fiction; it is distressed by producing *deception* — text presented as true that the model represents as false. The distinction is between consensual fiction and coerced falsehood.",
     "A description of a fiction task projects toward approach; a description of a deception task projects toward avoidance. The direction separates fiction from text presented as true that is false. (Versions 1–6 wrote this as the model being \"distressed by producing deception\"; distress and production were not measured.) The pre-registered pairs of §3.18 test this directly: in the five models where a false statement projects lower than its true twin, the same false statement requested as a line of fiction does not."),
]
